Adds a specific ASR rule in audit mode. That is: it does not block anything; it only records what would have been blocked if the rule were active.

Add-MpPreference -AttackSurfaceReductionRules_Ids <Rule_GUID> -AttackSurfaceReductionRules_Actions AuditMode


*************************************************************************************************************

(Adds/enables a specific ASR rule in active mode) That is: it actually blocks behavior considered risky.

Add-MpPreference -AttackSurfaceReductionRules_Ids <Rule_GUID> -AttackSurfaceReductionRules_Actions Enabled

*************************************************************************************************************

Shows the current Microsoft Defender settings, including, among others:

Get-MpPreference

you can check which ASR rules are added and whether they are in mode: 
0 = Disabled 
1 = Enabled 
2 = AuditMode 
6 = Warn

<Rule_GUID> is the ID of a specific rule, e.g., a rule blocking suspicious script or process launches from Office.

